<p>Were we to take bets on the single most widely heard alarm clock, the one included on the Beatles’ “<a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=usNsCeOV4GM">A Day in the Life</a>” would surely be a popular choice. It comes, you’ll recall, at the beginning of the song’s middle section, when Paul takes over from John and sings about waking up, combing his hair, catching a bus, and having a smoke. Given the lyrics’ subject matter, its inclusion seems natural, and even like one of the most straightforwardly explicable choices in the entire recording. In fact, the alarm clock was never meant to stay in the mix: the band’s assistant Mal Evans set it off as a gag after doing a placeholder count of 24 measures in the middle of the sessions, but it just happened to work perfectly with the form the song eventually took.</p>
<p>That’s just one of the fortuitous accidents music YouTuber David Hartley recounts in <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WPKHKkylciI">the video above</a>, on the complete making of “A Day in the Life.” As most Beatles fans know, John <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2026/02/how-the-beatles-wrote-their-songs-from-early-demos-to-final-recordings.html">really did take his initial inspiration</a> from the newspaper, and in particular a couple of articles about a Guinness heir’s fatal car crash and 4,000 holes in Blackburn, Lancashire.</p>
<div class="oc-center-da">
<div data-fuse="22871471544"></div>
</div>

<p>But after he wrote his verses, he found himself at a loss as to how to develop the song from there. Paul offered the bit he’d already written about falling out of bed and dragging a comb across his head. It wasn’t long before the Beatles began recording — and doing so for the first time, Hartley notes, before having finished writing the song. They had disparate pieces of music to join together, but no idea, yet, of how they’d do it.</p>
<p>Hence those 24 empty bars. Playing a series of early takes, Hartley shows how they got to be filled in, drawing heavily on <a href="https://amzn.to/4gRYz4Z"><em>Here, There, and Everywhere: My Life Recording the Music of the Beatles</em></a>, the memoir of engineer Geoff Emerick. John’s idea of “a sound that began really small, and then gradually expanded into something huge” inspired Paul’s suggestion of a symphony orchestra, in response to which producer <a href="https://www.openculture.com/2021/12/how-george-martin-defined-the-sound-of-the-beatles.html">George Martin</a> expressed concerns about the cost of hiring one. Ringo, in turn, jokingly proposed hiring half the orchestra and having them play the part twice, which turned out to be a pragmatic solution. As for how to end a song like this, the use of “maybe the most famous single chord in recorded history” — followed by “a high-frequency tone only dogs could hear and a short loop of nonsense sound” —&nbsp;came as the result of a process we can only call Beatlesque.</p>
